.TH KOSH 1 "2026-10-25" "kosh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kosh \- a fast and debuggable POSIX- and Bash-compatible command shell,
formatter, and language server
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBkosh\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fIscript\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]]
.br
\fBkosh \-c\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
\fBkosh \-s\fR [\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
\fBkosh \-\-lint\fR [\fB\-c\fR \fIcommand\fR ...] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.br
\fBkosh \-\-format\fR [\fIfile\fR]
.br
\fBkosh\fR [\fB\-\-lint\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR] \fB\-\-apply\fR \fIfile\fR ...
.br
\fBkosh \-\-as\-language\-server\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kosh
is the Koshka shell: an interpreter, an interactive shell, a formatter, a
language server, and a diagnostic tool. It accepts full POSIX shell and Bash
syntax with Bash-compatible extensions, and it runs on Linux, macOS, and
Windows with the same grammar. On Windows an unquoted backslash is an escape,
so a native path needs a quoted or escaped backslash or a forward slash.
.SH INVOCATION
The input source is selected in this order:
.B \-s
selects standard input, one or more
.B \-c
options select their command strings, a script operand selects that file, and
.B \-i
or a terminal on standard input selects the interactive editor. Otherwise the
shell reads standard input.
.PP
The invocation basename selects an initial mood:
.B sh
or
.B dash
selects the
.B sh
mood, and
.B bash
or
.B rbash
selects the
.B bash
mood.
.B rbash
also requests restricted mode. A dash at the start of argument zero, or
.BR \-l ,
selects login operation.
.PP
.B KOSH_FLAGS
supplies options before the command line, and a command-line option overrides
the same option from it.
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-\-format ,
.BR \-\-apply ,
.BR \-\-as\-language\-server ,
.BR \-\-rcfile ,
.BR \-\-init\-file ,
and every operand are ignored there. A secure execution ignores and removes
.BR KOSH_FLAGS .
.PP
When argument zero begins with a dash, the options cannot be parsed, and
standard input is a terminal, the shell enters rescue mode: it clears the
options and skips startup files, and it stays restricted when an option
requested restricted mode. Any other invalid invocation exits with status 2.
.SH OPTIONS
An option given on the command line, on or off, overrides the settings files
and
.BR KOSHCONF .
.SS General options
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and notices, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-short\-version
Print the short version, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print help, then exit.
.SS POSIX options
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Run interactively.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Read commands from standard input. This takes precedence over command strings
and file operands.
.TP
.BR \-c " \fICOMMAND\fR, " \-\-command =\fICOMMAND\fR
Run COMMAND. The option may be repeated, and the strings run in order. A
string with a syntax error ends the shell with status 2, one that analysis
rejects ends it with status 1, and the later strings do not run. Command
strings and a script operand take precedence over
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-error\-exit
Exit after an unguarded failing command.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-glob
Disable pathname expansion.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-one\-command
Read and run one command, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each input line as it is read.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-xtrace
Print each command after expansion.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-export\-all
Mark each later assignment for export.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-no\-clobber
Keep a plain output redirection from overwriting an existing regular file.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-exec
Parse and analyze commands without running them. A compatibility mood reports
only the tiers that
.B \-W
selects.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-no\-unset
Treat the expansion of an unset variable as an error.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-login
Act as a login shell.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-m
Accepted without effect.
.TP
.BR \-o " \fINAME\fR, " +o " \fINAME\fR"
Enable or disable an option as
.B set \-o
and
.B set +o
do.
.B \-o posix
acts as
.BR \-\-posix .
An unknown option exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR +a ", " +C ", " +e ", " +f ", " +n ", " +p ", " +t ", " +u ", " +v ", " +x
Turn the option of that letter off, as
.B set +e
does.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing. The next operand is the script.
.SS Bash options
.TP
.BR \-\-init\-file " \fIFILE\fR, " \-\-rcfile " \fIFILE\fR"
Use FILE as the interactive rc file in place of ~/.bashrc in an interactive
non-login Bash mood, and in place of the user rc script in an interactive
non-login
.B kosh
mood.
.TP
.B \-\-norc
Skip the interactive non-login Bash rc files, the user rc script, and a custom
rc file. Login profiles and BASH_ENV are unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-O " \fINAME\fR, " +O " \fINAME\fR"
Turn the shopt option NAME on or off, as
.B shopt \-s
and
.B shopt \-u
do. An unknown option exits with status 2.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-restricted
Become restricted after the startup files finish.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-privileged
Enable privileged mode: BASH_ENV is not sourced, KOSHCONF is ignored and
removed, and no settings file is read. A secure execution, such as a setuid or
setgid invocation, always drops the elevated ids and skips every startup and
settings file. On Linux, such a shell whose real user is not root also clears
its capability sets, including the ambient set, and aborts if it can still
switch to root.
.TP
.B \-\-posix
Select the
.B bash-posix
mood.
.SS Compatibility options
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-mood =\fINAME\fR
Select the mood:
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
.BR sh ,
or
.BR bash-posix .
Without this option, the first line of a script file operand selects the
mood: a bash shebang selects
.BR bash ,
an sh or dash shebang selects
.BR sh ,
and a kosh shebang selects
.BR kosh ,
directly or through
.BR env .
Any other interpreter, or no shebang, leaves the
.B kosh
mood.
.BR \-\-mood ,
.BR \-\-dumb ,
.BR \-\-posix ,
and an invocation basename such as sh or bash take precedence over the
shebang. The shebang is not used for
.BR \-c ,
standard input, or
.BR \-\-lint .
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-init\-moods =\fILIST\fR
Source the startup files of each mood in LIST, in order. Moods are separated
by commas, or the option is repeated.
Without this option, an interactive or login shell uses init_moods from the
settings, and any other shell uses the selected mood.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-enable\-mimicry
Run a script started as a command under the mood its shell shebang selects, by
the precedence rules of
.BR \-\-mood .
A script operand always does so.
.TP
.B \-\-dumb
Apply
.BR "\-\-mood sh" ,
.BR \-\-no\-completion ,
.BR "\-\-tab\-selector plain" ,
and
.BR \-\-no\-diagnostics ,
and disable color. An explicit
.B \-\-tab\-selector
overrides the plain selector.
.SS Auxiliary options
.TP
.B \-\-lint
Act as
.B \-n
with every diagnostic tier enabled at its normal severity, overriding
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
and
.BR \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics ,
and skip every startup file. Each command string is analyzed in order, then
each file operand, or standard input when neither is given.
.IP
Shell commands are also extracted from CI configurations, Ansible, Dockerfile,
Compose, Markdown, Makefile, justfile, Kubernetes, and similar hosts, and only
the extracted sh, bash, or kosh source is checked. A host interpreter setting,
such as a step shell, a SHELL assignment or instruction, a shebang, or an
executable option, selects the shell, and a command for another interpreter is
skipped. An exec array contributes only the script of a shell
.B \-c
command. A justfile recipe line, joined across backslash continuations, is one
command, and a shebang or script recipe is one script. A Jinja template of a
shell script, such as
.IR name .sh.j2,
is checked with its template syntax masked. Host template expressions and
editor variables are masked in every format, interpolated values count as
plain words, and an unresolved command is not reported in a fragment that holds
an interpolation. Variables that the host provides, such as CI variables,
exported recipe parameters, and Dockerfile ARG and ENV values, count as set.
Diagnostics point into the host file.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-apply ,
safe structured fixes are written to each file, and only the diagnostics that
remain are reported. A warning-only result exits with status 0, and a remaining
error or an input or write failure exits with status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-format
Format shell source without running it: structural keywords and compound
braces go on separate lines, each nested level indents by two spaces, direct
.B test
commands become bracket commands unless an earlier alias or function shadows
them, code wraps at 80 columns at a safe token boundary, and lines end in LF.
With no file or a lone
.BR \- ,
standard input is formatted; otherwise one file is formatted to standard
output. Combined with
.BR \-\-lint ,
the lint fixes are applied first.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Update file operands with safe lint fixes under
.BR \-\-lint ,
formatted source under
.BR \-\-format ,
or both. A changed file is replaced atomically, and its mode and symbolic links
are kept. Standard input,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-c ,
and a lone
.B \-
are rejected.
.TP
.B \-\-as\-language\-server
Run the language server protocol over standard input and output. The server
provides diagnostics, quick fixes, a fix-all action for the non-conflicting
safe fixes, completion, navigation, command information, semantic tokens, an
outline, and rename. It works inside the shell regions of the host formats
that
.B \-\-lint
recognizes. Rename updates variables, functions, and aliases in the open
document and keeps the sigil and braces of
.B $name
and
.BR ${name} ;
a command can be renamed only when the document defines it as a function or
alias. A recognized shebang selects the mood, then the client language
identifier, then the document extension, and otherwise the
.B kosh
mood. File operands,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-\-lint ,
.BR \-\-format ,
and
.B \-\-apply
are rejected.
.SS Koshka options
.TP
.BR \-Q ", " \-\-no\-init\-files
Read no startup file, and set PATH to /usr/bin:/bin.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Read no settings file, and ignore and remove KOSHCONF. PATH and the startup
files are unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-WW ", " \-WWW
Set the analysis level. Analysis has strict, lenient, and annoying tiers. In
the
.B kosh
mood, strict and lenient findings are errors and annoying findings are
warnings;
.B \-W
keeps that,
.B \-WW
demotes lenient errors to warnings, and
.B \-WWW
also demotes strict errors. In the other moods no analysis runs until a level
is set, and
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-WW ,
and
.B \-WWW
enable the strict, the lenient, and the annoying tier as warnings, each with
the tiers before it. A level set with
.B set
passes to launched shells through
.BR KOSH_ANALYSIS .
Runtime diagnostics do not depend on the level.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-diagnostics
List the analysis checks and their metadata, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-diagnostics
Skip analysis and every warning and pre-run diagnostic.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-annoying\-diagnostics
Suppress the annoying tier.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-init\-diagnostics
Suppress diagnostics and warnings while startup files run.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-traces
Suppress source backtraces (optimizer.show_source_traces off).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-completion
Disable tab completion, ghost text, and syntax coloring (completion.on_tab
off).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-syntax\-highlighting
Disable syntax coloring and ghost text and keep tab completion
(editor.highlight_syntax_and_show_ghost_text off).
.TP
.BR \-\-tab\-selector " \fIMODE\fR"
Select how an explicit Tab presents several candidates and how Ctrl\-R presents
the history:
.B interactive
(the default),
.BR external ,
or
.BR plain .
See
.BR "LINE EDITING" .
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-koshkit
Resolve bundled utilities directly as commands, as
.B koshconf set interpreter.resolve_koshkit_applets_as_commands on
does.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic
Enable arbitrary-precision integers and finite decimal values, as
.B koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers on
does. The
.B kosh
mood keeps this on.
.SS Debug options
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-show\-ast
Print syntax trees before execution and during formatting or linting.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-optimizer\-diagnostics
Report each constant-folded expression and eliminated branch as a lenient
diagnostic, which a compatibility mood shows only under
.BR \-WW .
.TP
.B \-\-show\-exit\-code
Print a located diagnostic when a command returns a nonzero status
(debug.report_nonzero_exit_codes).
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes
Report every command status, including zero.
.B set \-N
and
.B set +N
toggle it.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-show\-lexed\-words
Print the escape map of each parsed command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-stats
Print statistics on commands, processes, input and output, and resource usage
after each command.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-memory
Print memory usage at exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-ftrace ", " \-\-strace
Enable function or system-call tracing in a Cosmopolitan build.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I script
When neither
.B \-s
nor
.B \-c
selects another source, the first operand is the script file. A lone
.B \-
selects standard input. A directory fails with status 126, and a file that
cannot be opened fails with status 127.
.TP
.I argument
For a script, the script path becomes $0 and later arguments become positional
parameters. For
.BR \-c ,
the first operand after COMMAND becomes $0. For
.B \-s
and interactive operation, $0 stays the invocation name and every operand
becomes a positional parameter.
.SH MOODS
The
.B kosh
mood is the default. It enables strict checks and analysis and holds nounset,
pipefail, failglob, and extended arithmetic on and nullglob off.
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
and
.B koshconf
reject any other value of these with a hint to use
.BR "koshconf set mood bash" ,
but accept the value already held, so
.B set \-euo pipefail
still runs. Every other option can be toggled.
.PP
The
.B bash
mood accepts Bash extensions with analysis off by default. The
.B bash-posix
mood is Bash syntax with the POSIX option on, and sources the Bash startup
files. The
.B sh
mood follows dash. In these moods every Bash option can be toggled, and an
explicit
.BR "set \-u" ,
.BR "set \-o pipefail" ,
.BR "shopt \-s failglob" ,
or
.B koshconf set interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
survives a later change between them.
.B set \-o posix
selects
.BR bash-posix ,
and
.B set +o posix
selects
.B bash
from the
.B bash-posix
or
.B sh
mood.
.PP
An error that ends a non-interactive shell, such as an unset parameter under
.B set \-u
or ${name?word} with name unset, passes through an eval and a sourced file, as
in Bash and dash. A subshell, a command substitution, a trap action, and a
startup file contain it.
.PP
On Windows,
.B echo
in the
.B kosh
mood leaves backslashes literal unless
.B \-e
is given, and the
.B sh
mood keeps dash escape handling. A path of /dev/null opens the null device, and
/tmp or a path under it maps into the directory in TEMP; the options
compatibility.dev_null and compatibility.slash_tmp, both on, govern the two
mappings.
.SH OPTION REGISTRY
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR koshconf ,
the
.B \-o
test, SHELLOPTS, and BASHOPTS share one registry. Each option has a numeric id,
a koshconf name, a type, a class, and possibly a Bash spelling and a letter. An
interactive option affects only the editor, the history, or the presentation
of diagnostics; a semantic option can change what a script does.
.PP
A koshconf name starts with its topic:
.B mood
and
.B init_moods
(mood selection),
.B interpreter
(shell behavior),
.B compatibility
(Windows path mapping),
.B editor
(the line editor, with
.B editor.history
and
.BR editor.hints ),
.BR completion ,
.BR debug ,
or
.B optimizer
(analysis). The name of an option from POSIX
.B set \-o
starts with posix, and one from another Bash
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
option starts with bash, as in posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure for
errexit. Every other option exists only in Koshka.
.PP
An option with a Bash spelling is also accepted as posix.legacy.NAME when POSIX
.B set \-o
accepts NAME, and as bash.legacy.NAME otherwise, as in bash.legacy.extglob. The
alias is the same option, and every option operand and kosh.conf line accepts
it. Error messages, KOSHCONF,
.BR "koshconf create" ,
and
.B koshconf list
use only the canonical name;
.B koshconf list \-\-all
adds each alias.
.PP
A settings file may set every option except those fixed by the invocation or
the session. bash.interpreter.login_shell and bash.interpreter.restricted_shell
are read-only. bash.interpreter.privileged_mode,
posix.interpreter.parse_without_executing, and
bash.interpreter.exit_after_one_command come from the command line.
posix.interpreter.job_control, bash.editor.history.records_commands,
bash.editor.history.bang_expansion, and bash.interpreter.posixly_correct follow
the session and the mood.
.PP
editor.base_mode covers the Bash options emacs and vi: it is vi while
.B set \-o vi
is on and emacs otherwise, and
.BR "set \-o emacs" ,
.BR "set \-o vi" ,
the
.B \-o
test, and SHELLOPTS behave as in Bash. The Bash options hashall and nolog have
no koshconf name;
.B set
and
.B \-o
accept them. The bash.legacy shopt options in the table change nothing, so Bash
startup files that set them run unchanged; Koshka always appends to the history
file and records a multi-line command as one entry. shopt accepts
array_expand_once as the Bash 5.3 spelling of assoc_expand_once.
.PP
In the table, i marks an interactive option and s a semantic one.
.PP
