.PP
mood takes kosh, sh, bash, or bash-posix. completion.menu_style takes
interactive, external, or plain. optimizer.warning_level takes 0 to 3.
editor.base_mode takes emacs or vi. editor.history.file_path and
editor.history.max_entries correspond to KOSH_HISTORY_FILE and
KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE. editor.wrap_width is an integer from 0 to 65535, 80 by
default. init_moods is a comma-separated list of the moods
.B \-L
accepts, empty by default; it applies only to an interactive or login shell at
startup, yields to
.BR \-L ,
and a restricted shell ignores it in the settings files.
.SH STARTUP FILES
Every shell applies the settings files before any startup file: the system
file /etc/kosh.conf (%ProgramData%\ekosh\ekosh.conf on Windows), then the user
file $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf or ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf, then a
.B KOSHCONF
variable, which is then removed from the environment. These files hold
settings, not shell code;
.BR kosh (5)
describes them. A mood set there selects the startup files below unless the
command line or the invocation name selects one, and options given on the
command line keep their values.
.PP
A login or interactive shell in the
.B kosh
mood sources ~/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh and no system rc file. A Bash
startup file that the script sources runs in the
.B bash
mood, as
.BR kosh (5)
describes.
.B \-L bash
on the command line, or init_moods in a settings file, makes a
.B kosh
session source the Bash startup files in the
.B bash
mood first and then return to the
.B kosh
mood. Every mood sets KOSH without exporting it, so a line guarded by
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in Koshka.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B sh
mood sources /etc/profile and ~/.profile. An interactive
.B sh
shell sources the file in ENV, without a PATH search, unless privileged mode is
on.
.PP
A login shell in the
.B bash
or
.B bash-posix
mood sources /etc/profile, then the first readable file of ~/.bash_profile,
~/.bash_login, and ~/.profile. An interactive non-login shell in those moods
sources the first readable file of /etc/bash/bashrc and /etc/bash.bashrc, then
the custom rc file or ~/.bashrc, then
/usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion. A non-interactive shell in the
.B bash
mood sources BASH_ENV once, without a PATH search;
.B bash-posix
does not.
.PP
Clean
.RB ( \-Q ),
rescue, lint, format, and language server shells skip every startup and
settings file, as does a shell whose real and effective ids differ. Privileged
mode suppresses ENV and BASH_ENV. Restricted mode and the strictness of the
mood take effect after startup. A missing startup file is skipped, and an
unreadable custom rc or BASH_ENV file is also reported.
.SH RESTRICTED SHELL
A restricted shell rejects directory changes; changes to SHELL, PATH, ENV, and
BASH_ENV; a directory separator in a command word or a source operand; the
redirections >, >|, <>, >>, &>, &>>, and >&;
.BR exec ,
.BR newgrp ,
.BR bench ,
.BR assimilate ,
and
.BR slurp ;
.BR "hash \-p" ,
.BR "command \-p" ,
.BR "enable \-f" ,
.BR "enable \-d" ,
.BR "set \-M" ,
.BR "set \-I" ,
.BR "set +I" ,
.BR "set +r" ,
.BR "source \-\-mood" ,
history file operands, every form of
.BR koshkit ,
and the
.B koshconf
forms
.BR create ,
.BR set ,
.BR load ,
and
.BR "save \-\-persist" .
Numeric descriptor duplication stays available.
.PP
A command word falls back only to a bundled utility that a startup file enabled
with
.BR enable .
After startup,
.B enable
can disable a builtin but cannot enable one or change a bundled utility. An
enabled utility that starts other programs, such as
.BR env ,
.BR xargs ,
.BR find ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR timeout ,
or
.BR watch ,
can start any program, including another shell, so a startup file for a
restricted shell should enable only utilities that start none.
.PP
No setting turns restricted mode off. A shell started restricted by
.B \-r
or as rbash ignores and removes
.B KOSHCONF
with a warning, and applies only presentation and diagnostic options from the
settings files; it ignores the mood, a semantic option,
editor.history.file_path, and editor.history.max_entries there with a warning.
A child shell inherits the restrictions, and a sourced script keeps them. A
shell script found through PATH and run as a command runs without them.
.SH SHELL GRAMMAR
The shell accepts the POSIX Shell Command Language and the Bash extensions
described below. The
.B sh
mood disables Bash-only syntax except for documented compatibility forms. An
if command with no selected branch and no else returns status 0.
.SS Coprocesses
.EX
coproc [NAME] command [redirections]
.EE
.PP
Every mood except
.B sh
accepts a coprocess. The command runs asynchronously, connected to the shell by
two pipes: NAME[0] is read from, NAME[1] is written to, and NAME_PID holds the
process id. NAME defaults to COPROC, and a word before the command is NAME only
when a compound command follows it. The launch returns status 0. See also
.BR "JOB CONTROL" .
.SH QUOTING
Single quotes preserve every enclosed byte. Double quotes keep parameter,
command, and arithmetic expansion. A backslash quotes the next byte where the
grammar permits it. $'...' interprets C escapes such as \en, \et, \exHH, and
\eOOO. $"..." marks a string for translation and, without a catalog, acts as a
double-quoted string.
.SH PARAMETERS
.SS Positional parameters
An unquoted $@ or $* is subject to field splitting. "$@" keeps one field per
parameter, and "$*" joins the parameters with the first byte of IFS. A $@ that
becomes one string, as in an assignment, joins with the first byte of IFS in
the
.B sh
mood and with a space in the Bash moods. In the
.B sh
mood, as in dash, $@ and $* are always set, so "${@\-d}" and "${@+p}" never
test for missing parameters.
.SS Indexed arrays
a=(x y z) assigns an array, a[2]=z an element, and a+=(w) appends. ${a[i]}
takes an arithmetic subscript, and a negative index counts from the end.
${a[@]} and ${a[*]} list elements, ${#a[@]} counts them, and ${!a[@]} lists the
indices that are set. "${a[@]}" keeps one field per element, "${a[@]@A}" yields
the words of the declaration, and "${a[*]}" joins with the first byte of IFS.
An unquoted ${a[@]} or ${a[*]} joins with the first byte of IFS and splits the
result. An unset array in ${a[@]} is an error under
.BR "set \-u" .
.SS Associative arrays
.B declare \-A m
declares an associative array, m[key]=v assigns an element, ${m[key]} expands
it, ${!m[@]} lists keys, and
.B unset 'm[key]'
removes one. Keys are listed in the order first assigned, which may differ from
Bash, so a script that depends on an order should sort them.
.PP
.B export
and
.B declare \-x
on an array keep the array and record the export attribute, which
.B declare \-p
prints as \-ax or \-Ax. As in Bash, no child receives the array.
.SH EXPANSIONS
.SS Parameter expansion
${v:offset:length} selects a substring. A negative offset needs a leading
space, as in ${v: \-3}, since ${v:\-3} is the default-value form. A negative
length excludes that many characters from the end. On an indexed array,
${a[@]:offset:length} starts at the first element whose index is at least the
offset.
.PP
${v/pat/rep} replaces the first match and ${v//pat/rep} every match; /# and /%
anchor at the start and end. While the
.B patsub_replacement
option is on, the default, an unquoted ampersand in the replacement, or one
from an unquoted expansion, stands for the match, and a quoted ampersand or \e&
is literal. With the option off, every ampersand is literal.
.PP
${v^^}, ${v,,}, ${v^}, and ${v,} change case, with an optional pattern.
${v@Q}, ${v@E}, ${v@A}, ${v@P}, and ${v@a} yield a quoted value, expanded
escapes, an assignment, a prompt expansion, or the attributes, and ${v@U},
${v@L}, and ${v@u} convert case. Any other operator after @ on a set parameter
is a bad substitution. A value or key with a byte that does not print in the
locale, such as a control byte or invalid UTF-8, takes the $'...' form in
${v@Q}, ${v@A}, ${a[@]@K}, printf %q, declare \-p, and export \-p.
.PP
${!ref} expands the variable whose identifier ref holds, and ${!pre*} and
${!pre@} list the variables beginning with pre.
.PP
Inside double quotes or a here-document, the word of ${v:\-word}, ${v:+word},
and ${v:=word}, with or without the colon, follows double-quote rules, so
"${v:\-'a'}" expands to 'a' with its quotes.
.SS Arithmetic expansion
Arithmetic accepts the C operators, ++, \-\-, **, the conditional and comma
operators, and assignment operators. Literals may use 0x hexadecimal, 0 octal,
0b binary (a Koshka extension; Bash writes 2#), or a base prefix such as 16#ff.
A variable value is evaluated as an arithmetic expression without further
expansion, so a $ or a backquote in it is an error; a subscript taken from a
value is expanded again, as in Bash, so q='a[$i]' and $((q)) yield the element
at i. An empty (( )) returns 1, and an empty $(( )) yields 0. Outside the
.B sh
mood, double quotes inside $(( )), (( )), a C-style for header, a subscript,
or a substring offset are removed first, so $(( "1 + 2" * 3 )) is 7, and
$[expression] is the obsolete spelling of $((expression)). The
.B sh
mood rejects every quote there and keeps $[ literal.
.PP
The
.B interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers
option selects arbitrary-precision integers and finite decimal literals. The
.B kosh
mood holds it on, and the other moods start with it off, in which case signed
64-bit arithmetic wraps.
.SS Expansion errors
In the
.B kosh
mood every arithmetic and expansion error below ends the script with status 1.
.PP
In the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods an arithmetic error in $(( )), a subscript, a substring offset, or an
assignment to an integer variable abandons the rest of the input line, as in
Bash: the remaining commands on the line do not run, $? is 1 on the next line,
and neither
.B set \-e
nor an ERR trap acts on it. An eval, a sourced file, a subshell, and a command
substitution count as separate lines. An error in (( )),
.BR let ,
a [[ ]] operand, or a C-style for header fails only that command with status
1. Outside a here-document body, a $(( )) error ends a non-interactive shell
with status 1 in the
.B bash-posix
mood and 2 in the
.B sh
mood; an interactive shell abandons the line instead.
.PP
Other expansion errors follow Bash 5.3. A glob matching nothing under failglob
abandons the line in the
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods. A bad substitution, such as ${x!y} or ${x:}, and an assignment to a
read-only variable abandon the line in the
.B bash
mood and end a non-interactive shell in the
.B bash-posix
and
.B sh
moods. An unset parameter under
.B set \-u
and ${NAME?word} end the script in every mood.
.PP
In the
.B sh
mood, an error of a regular builtin, such as a missing cd directory or an
invalid test operand, fails that command with status 2 and the script goes on.
An error of a special builtin or of local, such as shift past the last
parameter or export of an invalid identifier, ends a non-interactive shell with
status 2 unless
.B command
runs it. printf without a format fails with status 2 in every mood.
.SS Command substitution
$(command) and `command` expand to the output of the command with trailing
newlines removed. The Bash and
.B sh
moods also drop null bytes, and the
.B kosh
mood keeps them. A command substitution restores shell state, options, the
directory stack, the working directory, and the file creation mask when it
finishes. A syntax error inside a command, process, or function substitution is
reported at its place in the outer source, and the script or command string
holding it does not run. Nesting deeper than 64 levels is a syntax error.
Analysis checks a command, backtick, or process substitution as a subshell, and
a function substitution in the current shell.
.SS Process substitution
<(command) and >(command) expand to a descriptor path, with the command in a
separate process. The form expands anywhere in an unquoted word, so
p=<(command) assigns the path. The path stays open until the command that
expanded it finishes, or until the loop finishes for a path in the word list of
a for loop; the shell then closes it and waits for the process, where Bash
leaves it running. A process substitution sets
.B $!
to its process, and
.B wait
on that process returns its status. On POSIX the path is /dev/fd/63 or the next
free descriptor above it. On Windows it is a pipe that acts as a FIFO: each
reader continues where the previous one stopped, and each writer appends to the
same stream.
.SS Brace expansion
{a,b,c} produces alternatives, and {1..10}, {a..z}, and {1..10..2} produce
ranges. A numeric range such as {001..10} keeps its width. Braces nest, and a
lone {a} stays literal.
.SS Field splitting
IFS controls field splitting. A run of IFS whitespace collapses, and each
non-whitespace IFS byte delimits a field, which may be empty.
.SS Pathname expansion
The Bash moods sort matches by the collation order of the locale, and the
.B kosh
and
.B sh
moods by byte value.
.B extglob
enables ?(), *(), +(), @(), and !() matching, which the pattern of
.B [[
always accepts.
.B globstar
enables **.
.BR dotglob ,
.BR nullglob ,
and
.B failglob
control leading dots, nonmatches, and failed matches. In the
.B kosh
mood a nonmatching glob is fatal; in a compatibility mood with failglob off it
stays literal.
.PP
A nonempty
.B GLOBIGNORE
holds colon-separated patterns: a result matching one is removed, a glob whose
results are all removed follows the nonmatching rules, and . and .. are never
results. Assigning a nonempty GLOBIGNORE enables
.BR dotglob ,
and unsetting it disables
.BR dotglob .
.PP
The first nonempty value of LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, and LANG selects the character
locale. A UTF-8 or utf8 codeset selects UTF-8 when the platform supports it,
and anything else selects bytes. In UTF-8, a ? and each step of a * consume one
character, a bracket expression compares code points, ${#v} and
${v:offset:length} count characters, and ${v^}, ${v,}, and ${v~} change the
case of multibyte letters. This applies to pathname expansion, case, [[ == ]],
${v#pat}, ${v%pat}, ${v/pat/rep}, ${v^pat}, and compgen \-X. Completion always
matches by UTF-8 sequence.
.SH REDIRECTIONS
&> and &>> redirect standard output and standard error to one file, and |&
pipes both. <<< supplies a here-string followed by a newline. A descriptor
number before <<< or <<, as in 7<<< or 8<<EOF, selects the descriptor that
receives the input. {var}>, {var}<, {var}<<<, and {var}<< allocate a descriptor
numbered 10 or above and store its number in var, and {var}>&\- closes the
descriptor in var.
.PP
An appending redirection inside a loop body opens its file once for the whole
loop, so writes continue to the original file even after it is renamed or
removed; Bash reopens it on every iteration.
.PP
In the Bash moods, the redirections of a program, of a command with no word,
and of a subshell expand in the child, as in Bash, so a side effect such as
${v:=x} does not reach the shell. Those of a builtin, a function, a group, and
.B exec
expand in the shell, as do those of every command in the
.B kosh
mood.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
Command lookup checks functions, enabled builtins, cached program paths, PATH,
and, when the mood, the options, or
.B enable
allow them, bundled utilities. Running a command or
.B hash
caches its path, and a change to any PATH directory clears every cached path.
.PP
In an interactive shell, a single directory path in command position changes
to that directory as
.B cd
would, unless a function, builtin, program, or bundled utility of that name
exists. A word of only dots moves up one directory per dot after the first.
The
.B autocd
option controls this and is on in every mood; scripts are never affected.
.PP
A command path ending in a directory separator fails with status 126 when the
target is not a directory. A script without a recognized executable format runs
as a shell script without the caller's variables, functions, and traps.
.PP
PIPESTATUS holds the status of each stage of the last foreground pipeline.
Under
.BR pipefail ,
a pipeline returns the status of its rightmost failing stage. A pipeline stage
that expands to no command, such as
.BR "p=1 | cat" ,
is an error in the
.B kosh
mood; the other moods apply its redirections and return 0.
.SH FUNCTIONS
A function has its own positional parameters and local variables. FUNCNAME,
BASH_SOURCE, BASH_LINENO, and BASH_COMMAND expose the call stack. A function
called from a guarded and-or operand or a negation keeps
.B errexit
suppressed throughout its body.
.PP
In the
.B kosh
mood the RETURN trap fires at the end of every function body. The
.B bash
and
.B bash-posix
moods fire it for a body that
.B functrace
follows, and the
.B sh
mood rejects the RETURN condition.
.SH BUILTINS
The shell provides the POSIX and Bash builtins below, which follow the active
mood unless a difference is stated here. With no operand,
.B builtin
groups them under POSIX, BASH, and Koshka headings.
.PP
\fB: . [ alias bg bind break builtin caller cd command complete compgen compopt
continue declare dirs disown echo enable eval exec exit export false fc fg
getopts hash help history jobs kill let local logout mapfile newgrp popd printf
pushd pwd read readarray readonly return set shift shopt source suspend test
time times trap true type typeset ulimit umask unalias unset wait\fR
.SS Koshka builtins
.TP
\fBassimilate\fR [\fIoptions\fR] [\fITARGET\fR]
Copy the running executable through scp to TARGET and install it as
.B kosh
in the first usable remote PATH directory, skipping a path that contains sbin.
Without TARGET, install it the same way into the first usable local PATH
directory and create a relative symbolic link to
.B kosh
for each bundled utility and utility alias whose name is free there or already
links to it. On Windows the local form installs kosh.exe and makes a hard link
with the .exe suffix for each utility and link mood, and a later run with a
changed binary makes those links again. A name that another entry in that directory holds, or that a
program elsewhere on PATH provides, stays unlinked, and each such group is
reported in one warning.
.IP
.BI \-\-link\-mood= MOOD
adds a relative bash, dash, sh, or kosh link; it may be repeated and takes
comma-separated values.
.BI \-\-install\-directory= DIR
installs into DIR, even one containing sbin.
.BI \-\-ssh\-command= COMMAND
and
.BI \-\-scp\-command= COMMAND
replace ssh and scp and need TARGET.
.BR \-x ", " \-\-trace
prints progress. The shell asks for confirmation with the target and the full
path of the new binary, as described under
.BR "KOSHKIT UTILITIES" ;
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
skips the question. The copy is validated before the installed binary is
replaced, and a failed installation restores the previous file or link.
.TP
\fBbench\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fIcommand\fR ...
Run each command through
.B kosh \-c
and report the wall time, peak resident set, and available performance
counters as a mean, standard deviation, and range. Sampling stops after a
nonzero status unless
.B \-\-ignore\-exit\-code
is given.
.BR \-\-show\-output ,
.BR \-N " (" \-\-no\-shell ),
.BR \-n " (" \-\-runs ),
and
.BR \-t " (" \-\-duration )
control output, direct execution, the sample count, and the time budget in
milliseconds.
.BR \-w " (" \-\-warmup )
sets the number of unmeasured runs before sampling; without a value it runs one
to three until two runs agree within 10%, and without the option one
unmeasured run precedes sampling. A note follows the report when a sample is an
outlier.
.BR \-p " (" \-\-profile )
is
.BR "\-\-runs 1 \-\-extended" .
.IP
.BR \-E " (" \-\-extended )
also reports CPU time and usage, waiting time (wall time minus user and system
time, or the average active task count times wall time minus CPU time for a
parallel command), context switches, page faults, block operations, and read
and write calls and bytes. Each platform adds what it can measure: on Linux,
scheduler and hardware counters and, from one extra untimed traced run, system
calls, threads, processes, execs, and task counts; on macOS, processor
counters, run-queue wait, wakeups, energy, and storage figures; on Windows,
whole process-tree counters through a job object. Traced rows are marked
(traced), a row marked * counts only the measured process, and a row the
platform cannot report is omitted.
.TP
\fBkoshconf create\fR \fIPRESET\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
.PD 0
.TP
\fBkoshconf set\fR \fIOPTION VALUE\fR [\fB\-\-persist\fR]
.TP
\fBkoshconf get\fR \fIOPTION\fR
.TP
\fBkoshconf list\fR [\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR]
.TP
\fBkoshconf load\fR \fIENCODED\fR
.TP
\fBkoshconf save\fR [\fB\-\-persist\fR]
.PD
Read and change the Koshka settings.
.B create
writes the default file of the
.BR kosh ,
.BR bash ,
or
.B sh
preset to the user settings file, with every setting except the mood
commented out, and refuses to replace an existing file without
.BR \-\-force ;
it also writes the default user rc script when that is missing.
.B set
changes the option for the session, and with
.B \-\-persist
also rewrites its line in the user settings file, saving the previous file as
.IR kosh.conf.bak .
.B get
prints the value.
.B list
prints every option as
.IR name = value
in alphabetical order, and
.B \-a
adds a line for each alias.
.B load
applies a
.B KOSHCONF
value to the session.
.B save
prints the current mood and interactive options as a
.B KOSHCONF
value, so a kosh started with
.B KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)
adopts them; with
.BR \-\-persist ,
it writes the user settings file instead, holding each setting that differs
from the system file or, where the system file does not set it, from the
default of the mood.
.IP
A boolean is on or off, also accepted as true, false, 1, or 0. An unknown
option or value, or a file that cannot be read or written, fails with status
1, and a malformed form exits with status 2. An interactive shell reports the
file it wrote on standard error.
.TP
\fBslurp\fR [\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-V\fR] [\fB+\fILINE\fR] \fIFILE\fR ...
Edit each FILE in the line editor, one after another; a missing FILE starts
empty, and Ctrl\-C ends the whole run.
.BI + LINE
opens the next FILE at that line.
.BR \-e ", " \-\-emacs
selects the emacs key set,
.BR \-v ", " \-\-vi
the vi key set in insert mode, and
.BR \-V ", " \-\-vi\-normal
the vi key set in normal mode on the first line. The session runs on the
alternate screen. A shell script, recognized by its shebang or extension, keeps
syntax highlighting, and the format commands change only such a file.
.IP
The top row repeats the prompt and the command, the file fills the rows below,
and the last two rows show the file kind, line endings, size, and caret
position, then the key set and the last save result or the keys of the key
menu. A hint, a message, or a command line that needs more rows takes up to
eight while it shows. Enter starts a new line, and Up and Down do not recall history. Tab
inserts a tab at the start of a line or in a file that is not a shell script,
and completes otherwise. Tabs and trailing blanks are drawn shaded. The mouse
moves the caret, selects by dragging, and scrolls with the wheel. With the
emacs key set, Shift with a movement key also selects.
.IP
Ctrl\-X s, Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-S,
.BR :w ,
and
.B :write
save and keep the session open.
.BR :wq ,
.BR :wq! ,
.BR :wqa ,
.BR :wqa! ,
and
.B :x
save and leave when the save succeeds.
.BR :q ,
Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C, and Ctrl\-D on an empty buffer leave without saving, and
Ctrl\-C leaves with status 130. While the buffer has unsaved changes,
.B :q
refuses, the other keys and M\-x kill\-kosh must be pressed twice, and
.B :q!
leaves at once.
.BR : N
and
.B :$
move to line N and the last line. Ctrl\-S and Ctrl\-R, or
.B /
and
.BR ? ,
find text as it is typed.
.IP
A save writes a complete copy beside the file and renames it over the file,
keeping its mode and following a symbolic link. A file that another user owns,
a file with several links, or a file in a directory that neither the user nor
root owns, or that others can write without the sticky bit, is rewritten in
place, keeping its owner, group, and links, after a private backup copy is
made in the home or temporary directory; the copy is removed after a
successful write and reported after a failed one, and without a copy nothing
is written. A save is refused when another file has replaced the loaded one.
A failed save is reported and the edits stay open. CRLF line endings and the
final newline are kept.
.IP
An empty operand, a directory, a special or unreadable file, a file with a NUL
byte or invalid UTF-8, and a file over 16 MiB are refused with status 1, and
the next FILE still opens. A restricted shell, or standard input or output that
is not a terminal, is refused with status 1. A missing FILE, a
.BI + LINE
without a FILE, or more than one mode option exits with status 2.
.TP
\fBz\fR [\fIquery\fR ...]
Change to a directory that interactive shells have visited. A single operand
that is an existing directory is used as it is. Otherwise the operands form one
case-insensitive substring query, ranked by visit count and recency. With no
operand, z changes to the home directory.
.SS Standard builtins
.TP
\fBsource\fR [\fB\-\-mood\fR[\fB=\fR]\fIMOOD\fR] \fIFILE\fR
With
.BR \-\-mood ,
run FILE in MOOD in place of the mood its shebang or name selects, and
restore the previous mood afterwards. A startup file of another shell sourced
this way from rc.kosh runs without the
.B kosh
mood diagnostics.
.TP
.B pwd \-W
Print the physical working directory in Windows form with forward slashes, as
MSYS does; elsewhere act as
.BR "pwd \-P" .
.TP
.B dirs
Print the current directory and the saved stack. +N prints the Nth entry from
the top and \-N the Nth from the bottom.
.TP
.BR "pushd \-n" " \fIDIR\fR, " "popd \-n"
Add DIR below the current directory, or remove the entry below it, without
changing directory.
.TP
.B set
Without arguments, print the shell variables as sorted assignments. A minus
enables an option and a plus disables it.
.B set \-o
.I NAME
accepts only Bash spellings; without NAME,
.B set \-o
prints the states and
.B set +o
prints reusable commands. A long option such as
.B \-\-mood
is rejected with a pointer to
.BR koshconf .
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-T ,
and
.B \-B
control errtrace, history expansion, physical directories, functrace, and
brace expansion.
.B \-k
places assignment arguments in the command environment.
.B \-t
and onecmd stop after one top-level command.
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.B \-m
are accepted without effect.
.IP
The Koshka forms
.BI "set \-M " MOOD
and
.BI "set \-L " MOOD,...
select the mood and source the startup files of other moods as the
command-line options do; without a value they print the mood or the
initialized moods.
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-N ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-G
set interpreter.mimic_shebang, debug.print_syntax_tree,
debug.print_lexed_word_escapes, debug.report_every_exit_code,
debug.print_evaluation_statistics, and debug.print_memory_report_at_exit, and
.BR \-W ,
.BR \-WW ,
and
.B \-WWW
set optimizer.warning_level. Every other Koshka setting belongs to
.BR koshconf .
.IP
.B ignoreeof
makes an interactive shell exit only after IGNOREEOF consecutive end-of-file
inputs. Enabling it sets an unset IGNOREEOF to 10, assigning IGNOREEOF enables
it, and unsetting IGNOREEOF disables it.
.B nolog
is accepted and changes nothing.
.IP
The
.B history
option records submitted interactive commands, and
.B histexpand
expands history references; both are on in an interactive shell and off
otherwise. The events are
.BR !! ,
.BR !\-N ,
.BR !N ,
.BR !prefix ,
.BR !?text? ,
and
.BR !# .
The word designators are
.BR :^ ,
.BR :$ ,
.BR :* ,
.BR :N ,
.BR :N\-M ,
.BR :\-M ,
.BR :N* ,
.BR :N\-$ ,
and
.BR :N\- ,
and the modifiers are
.BR :h ,
.BR :t ,
.BR :r ,
and
.B :e
for paths;
.BI :s/ old / new /
for the first literal match,
.B :gs
and
.B :as
for every match, and
.B :Gs
for the first match in each word;
.B :&
to repeat the last substitution;
.B :q
and
.B :x
to quote the text as one argument or word by word; and
.B :p
to print and record the result without running it. A leading
.BI ^ old ^ new ^
substitutes in the previous event. Single-quoted and backslash-escaped
references stay literal. After an expansion error the command neither runs nor
enters the history.
.TP
\fBtime\fR [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-posix\fR] [\fB\-R\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]
Time a command or pipeline.
.B \-p
selects the POSIX report. The
.B kosh
mood adds a processor busy row and, where the platform supplies it, the peak
resident set of the command and its descendants;
.B \-R
always adds the resident set row. In the
.B sh
mood time is not a keyword and is looked up on PATH.
.TP
\fBenable\fR [\fB\-adnps\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fIname\fR ...]
Enable each builtin or bundled utility, or disable it with
.BR \-n .
A utility alias such as
.B egrep
is rejected and follows its utility. Each builtin word is separate, so
disabling
.B test
leaves
.B [
enabled. Lookup skips a disabled builtin and searches PATH and the bundled
utilities, and a disabled bundled utility never resolves as a command, though
the
.B koshkit
builtin still runs it. A disabled special builtin loses its special
properties. A subshell inherits the state, and its changes do not reach the
parent.
.IP
Without an operand,
.B enable
lists the enabled builtins and reachable utilities as
.B enable
commands,
.B \-n
the disabled ones, and
.B \-a
both;
.B \-s
limits a listing to special builtins. A builtin that the mood hides, such as
.B let
in the
.B sh
mood, is neither listed nor accepted. Koshka has no loadable builtins, so
.B \-f
with an operand and
.B \-d
fail.
.TP
.B hash
Remember or list command locations.
.B \-r
clears the cache, and
.B \-R
also searches PATH again.
.BI "\-p " path
records path for each command.
.B \-t
prints the remembered location of each command and fails for one with none.
.TP
\fBhistory\fR [\fB\-cnrwaps\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIselector\fR] [\fIoperand\fR]
.br
\fBhistory\fR \fB\-S\fR|\fB\-\-sync\fR
Without a maintenance option, list the events, limited to the newest N with a
numeric operand.
.B \-r
loads the history file, or the file operand, into the list, and
.B \-n
loads only events not yet loaded.
.B \-w
writes the list to the history file or replaces the file operand.
.B \-a
appends the events of the private list that the file lacks.
.B \-S
replaces the private list with a snapshot of the shared file.
.B \-c
clears the private list and leaves the file intact.
.B \-p
prints its operands without storing them, and
.B \-s
stores them as one event.
.B \-d
deletes an event number, a negative offset from the newest event, or an
inclusive range, and an interactive shell also removes it from the file.
.IP
Each shell loads its own private history list at startup. An interactive shell
appends each submitted command to the shared file at once, without importing
the commands of other running shells. A non-interactive shell writes the file
only through
.B history \-w
or
.BR "history \-a" .
.TP
\fBfc\fR [\fB\-e\fR \fIeditor\fR] [\fB\-lnr\fR] [\fIfirst\fR [\fIlast\fR]]
Edit and run history events. A number selects an event, a negative number is
relative to the current position, and text selects the newest event with that
prefix; the default is the previous event.
.B \-l
lists events instead, the previous sixteen by default,
.B \-n
omits numbers, and
.B \-r
reverses the order. The editor comes from
.BR \-e ,
FCEDIT, or EDITOR, and defaults to vi, or ed in the POSIX mood; an editor of
.B \-
runs the event unedited.
.B fc \-s
[\fIold\fR=\fInew\fR ...] [\fIcommand\fR]
applies each literal replacement to one event, prints it to standard error,
runs it, and returns its status.
.TP
.B bind
Accept options and operands without changing key bindings, and return 0.
.TP
.B caller
Without an operand, print nothing and return 1 at top level and 0 in a
function or sourced file. A number selects a call frame and prints its line and
source file.
.TP
.B help
Without a pattern, list the builtins. For a builtin, print the help of its
.B \-\-help
flag;
.B \-d
prints the short description,
.B \-s
the synopsis, and
.B \-m
a manpage-style page.
.TP
.B logout
In a login shell, run the EXIT trap and exit with the optional status. In
another shell, report an error and return 1.
.TP
.B suspend
Stop the shell until SIGCONT. A login shell refuses without
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BR declare ", " typeset
.B \-a
declares an indexed array,
.B \-A
an associative array,
.B \-i
an integer,
.B \-l
and
.B \-u
lowercase and uppercase conversion,
.B \-r
a read-only variable, and
.B \-x
an exported one; the plus forms remove the integer and case attributes.
.B \-f
and
.B \-F
print functions with or without their definitions.
.B \-p
prints declarations, and without operands
.B \-p
or an attribute option lists every variable with those attributes.
.B \-g
and
.B \-t
are accepted without effect.
.IP
.B \-n
makes a reference to the variable in its value, which may carry a subscript.
Expansion, assignment, append, export, and unset act on the target, following
chains, with the target's read-only, restricted, integer, and case rules.
${!name} gives the target,
.B unset \-n
and
.B +n
remove the reference, and [[ \-R name ]] is true for a reference with a value.
A reference without a value binds at its first assignment. A self-reference is
an error at top level, and a circular chain counts as unset with a warning. A
read-only variable, PATH, IFS, and a dynamic variable other than RANDOM and
SECONDS cannot become a reference.
.TP
.B local
Make variables local to the running function, with the attributes of
.BR declare .
.B local \-n
passes a variable of the caller by reference, and a
.B \-
operand saves the
.B set
options until the function returns.
.TP
.B readonly
Mark variables read-only.
.B \-p
lists them reusably,
.B \-a
and
.B \-A
treat operands as arrays, and
.B \-f
marks functions read-only, or lists them with their bodies when given alone.
.TP
.BR mapfile ", " readarray
Store input lines in an array.
.B \-t
strips delimiters,
.B \-n
limits the count,
.B \-s
skips leading lines,
.B \-O
sets the first index,
.B \-d
uses the first byte of its value as delimiter,
.B \-u
reads a descriptor, and
.B \-C
runs a callback with the index and record every
.B \-c
records, 5000 by default.
.TP
.B printf
.B \-v
assigns the result to a variable. The numeric locale is the first nonempty
value of LC_ALL, LC_NUMERIC, and LANG at each call: floating operands use its
decimal point, and the
.B \(aq
flag groups digits with its thousands separator. The C and POSIX locales and
every Windows locale use a dot and no grouping. A field width or precision over
16777216 is an error, and that conversion prints nothing.
.TP
.B read
.B \-r
keeps backslashes; otherwise a trailing backslash continues the line.
.B \-a
splits into an indexed array,
.B \-d
sets the delimiter (empty for NUL),
.B \-n
sets the maximum byte count,
.B \-N
reads exactly that many bytes unsplit, ignoring the delimiter,
.B \-t
sets a timeout in seconds,
.B \-p
prints a prompt at a terminal,
.B \-s
disables echo,
.B \-u
reads a descriptor, and
.B \-q
reads one byte and returns 0 for y or Y.
.B \-e
is accepted without effect.
.TP
.B compgen
.BI "\-V " array
stores the candidates in an indexed array and prints nothing; with no match
the array is empty and the status is 1.
.BI "\-P " prefix
and
.BI "\-S " suffix
add text to each candidate after prefix matching and
.B \-X
filtering.
.TP
.B complete
A spec offers the candidates of its actions, its
.B \-G
glob, its
.B \-W
words, and, on an explicit Tab, the COMPREPLY of its
.B \-F
function and the output lines of its
.B \-C
command, in that order. The
.B \-C
command runs as a command substitution with the command, the word, and the
previous word as arguments and COMP_LINE, COMP_POINT, COMP_KEY, and COMP_TYPE
exported. The ghost suggestion never runs a function or command. The actions
.BR \-abcdefgjksuv
and
.BI "\-A " action
list what
.B compgen
lists.
.B \-X
filters as
.B compgen \-X
does, and
.B \-P
and
.B \-S
add text.
.B \-o dirnames
adds matching directories when nothing else matched, and
.B \-o plusdirs
always.
A spec quotes inserted candidates only when it generates filenames, as in
Bash;
.B \-o fullquote
quotes every candidate,
.B \-o noquote
none,
.B \-o nosort
keeps the generated order, and
.B \-o nospace
adds no space. An empty result falls through to the next completion source. An
unknown
.B \-o
option or
.B \-A
action fails with status 2 and registers nothing.
.IP
With
.B progcomp_alias
on, a command without a spec uses the spec of the first word of its alias, and
an explicit Tab follows a PATH command to its symlink target. A command still
without a spec uses the
.B \-D
spec, and when its function returns 124 completion restarts with the spec it
registered.
.B \-E
serves an explicit Tab on an empty line and
.B \-I
the command word; their functions receive
.B _EmptycmD_
and
.B _InitialWorD_
as the command. Of several of
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
and
.BR \-I ,
the first in that order applies.
.B \-p
prints the specs in Bash order, and
.B \-r
removes the given specs, or all of them; a missing spec is an error.
.TP
.B compopt
.BI "\-o " option
turns a
.B complete \-o
option on and
.BI "+o " option
off, off winning a conflict. It applies to each given command's spec, or to the
first of the
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-E ,
and
.B \-I
specs, or, inside a completion function with neither, to the current
completion. Without
.B \-o
or
.BR +o ,
it prints one
.B compopt
line per spec. An unknown option fails with status 2, and a missing spec or,
outside a completion function, a missing target with status 1.
.TP
.B type
.B \-V
prints everything the shell holds about each word, such as a function's
definition site and body or the description of a builtin or utility.
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.B \-P
keep their terse output. The Bash moods report a function as "is a function"
followed by its body, and the other moods as "is a shell function".
.SH KOSHKIT UTILITIES
The
.B koshkit
builtin runs a bundled utility, and an unknown utility is an error. With no
utility it groups the catalog under POSIX and Koshka headings.
.B koshkit \-\-list
prints the utilities one per line, and
.B koshkit \-\-list\-flags
prints each utility and alias with its short and long flags.
.PP
The bundled set is basename, bc, cal, calc, cat, chgrp, chmod, chown, cksum,
cmp, comm, cp, csplit, cut, date, df, diff, dirname, du, env, evil, evildisk,
evilfiles, evilfs, evilio, eviliso, evillogs, evilnet, evilps, evilss, expand,
expr, file, find, flock, fold, fuser, getconf, goodcore, goodfsw, goodnode,
goodstat, grep, head, id, killall, less, link, ln, locale, logger, logname, ls,
make,
man, meow, mkdir, mkfifo, mknod, more, mv, nice, nl, nohup, nproc, od, paste,
pathchk, pkill, pr, printenv, ps, readlink, realpath, renice, retry, rm, rmdir,
sed, seq, sleep, slurp, sort, split, stat, strings, stty, sync, tabs, tail, tee,
timeout, touch, tput, tr, tsort, tty, uname, unexpand, uniq, unlink, watch, wc,
which, who, whoami, xargs, and yes, with the aliases egrep and fgrep for grep
and vi and emacs for slurp.
.PP
Every utility supports
.BR \-\-help ,
and
.B \-\-version
when invoked under its own name. Options may come before and after operands,
and
.B \-\-
ends them. calc, env, expr, flock, nice, nohup, retry, timeout, watch, and
xargs end their options at the first operand, so the command after it keeps its
own options. A utility with a styled report uses color only when its output is
a terminal, and redirected output holds no escape sequences.
.PP
A confirmation question, such as one from
.BR "rm \-i" ,
.BR "cp \-i" ,
.BR "mv \-i" ,
or
.BR assimilate ,
is written to standard error followed by [y/N] or [Y/n], the default in
capitals. An empty line takes the default, an answer beginning with y or Y
confirms, and any other answer or end of input declines.
.PP
When direct koshkit lookup is enabled, a PATH program takes precedence over a
bundled utility. Started under a utility name, the executable runs that
utility. Started as
.BR koshkit ,
the link that
.B make install
places beside
.BR kosh ,
it runs the utility in its first operand, as in
.BR "koshkit ls \-l" ,
and otherwise acts as the koshkit builtin. Either form skips shell startup,
settings, and KOSH_FLAGS.
.TP
.B basename
Print the final path component, minus an optional suffix.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-multiple
treats every operand as a path,
.BR \-s ", " \-\-suffix
removes a suffix from each and implies
.BR \-a ,
and
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
ends entries with NUL.
.TP
.B bc
Evaluate expressions, assignments, functions, arrays, control flow, input and
output bases, and scale.
.B \-l
adds atan, cos, exp, ln, and sin and sets scale to 20.
.TP
.B cal
Print a Gregorian month or year with weeks starting on Sunday.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-today
starts weeks on Monday and prints the date in words below.
.TP
.B calc
Evaluate arithmetic operands with arbitrary-precision integers and finite
decimals.
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
and
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pipe
read expressions interactively or from a pipe. The functions are abs, atan,
ceil, cmp, cos, exp, fact, fib, floor, frac, gcd, int, iseven, isint, isodd,
length, lcm, ln, max, min, scale, sgn, sin, and sqrt. Ctrl\-R searches the calc
history.
.TP
.B cat
Concatenate files or standard input.
.B \-n
numbers lines across files,
.B \-b
numbers nonempty lines,
.B \-s
squeezes empty runs,
.B \-v
shows control bytes in ^ notation and high bytes with M\-,
.B \-E
marks line ends with $ and a CR before a newline as ^M,
.B \-T
shows tabs as ^I, and
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.B \-A
act as
.BR \-vE ,
.BR \-vT ,
and
.BR \-vET .
.B \-u
is accepted.
.B \-\-syntax\-highlighting
colors shell source on a terminal.
.TP
.BR chgrp ", " chown
Change the group, or the owner and optional group, of each file.
.B \-R
recurses,
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-P
select which links are followed,
.B \-h
changes a link itself, and
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
keeps recursion on one file system.
.TP
.B chmod
Apply symbolic or octal modes.
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
recurses without following links, and
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
skips other file systems.
.TP
.B comm
Merge two sorted files into columns of lines only in the first, only in the
second, and in both;
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-2 ,
and
.B \-3
suppress them. A line out of order after an unpaired line is reported once per
file with status 1;
.B \-\-check\-order
stops at the first line out of order, and
.B \-\-nocheck\-order
skips the check, the later one winning.
.B \-\-output\-delimiter
sets the column separator (empty for NUL),
.B \-\-total
appends column counts, and
.B \-z
uses NUL line ends.
.TP
.B cp
Copy files and trees with
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.BR \-v .
A recursive copy keeps links as links;
.B \-L
follows every link,
.B \-H
only links on the command line, and
.B \-P
none. A link leading back into a directory being copied is reported as cyclic
and skipped with status 1.
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
copies a directory on another file system as an empty directory.
.TP
.B cut
Select bytes, characters, or fields with
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
or
.BR \-f .
.B \-d
sets the delimiter,
.B \-s
skips lines without one,
.B \-\-complement
inverts the selection,
.BI \-\-output\-delimiter= str
joins selections with str, and
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
uses NUL line ends.
.TP
.B df
Report mounted file systems or those of the operands in a right-aligned table.
.B \-k
uses 1024-byte blocks,
.B \-P
the POSIX header,
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
powers of 1024, and
.BR \-H ", " \-\-si
powers of 1000. Unreadable file systems are reported before the table.
.TP
.B diff
Compare two files line by line.
.B \-u
selects unified output,
.B \-w
ignores whitespace,
.B \-a
forces text, and up to two
.B \-L
options set the unified labels.
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
takes auto (the default), always, or never. The status is 0 for equal files, 1
for different files, and 2 for an error.
.TP
.B dirname
Print the directory part of each path.
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
ends entries with NUL.
.TP
.B du
Print every file and directory below each path, or the current directory, in
descending size order without following links.
.B \-s
prints totals only,
.B \-h
human-readable sizes,
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
skips other file systems,
.B \-\-tree
prints a tree, and
.BR \-T ", " \-\-top\-largest
a tree of the largest entries and their ancestors. Permission errors are
warnings, other read failures are errors, and either makes the status 1.
.TP
.B env
Run a command with temporary assignments, or print the environment.
.B \-i
starts empty, and each
.B \-u
removes a variable. The command is found through its PATH, or /usr/bin:/bin
without one.
.TP
.BR expand ", " unexpand
Convert tabs to spaces, or blanks to tabs.
.B \-t
takes ascending tab stops separated by commas or blanks, or one interval; a
last value /N adds a stop at each multiple of N, and +N one every N columns,
after the last explicit stop. The default is every eight columns.
.B "unexpand \-a"
also converts blanks after the first nonblank, and
.B \-t
implies it.
.TP
.BR evil " and the evil utilities"
.B evil
prints system identity, uptime, processor, memory, swap, file system, process,
and network information;
.B \-a
adds details,
.B \-s
selects identity fields,
.B \-u
lists local users, and
.B \-\-color
takes always, auto, or never.
.B evildisk
prints capacity, file system identity, and disk error and retry totals, and
with
.B \-a
labels, UUIDs, error counters, and SMART data where available.
.B evilfiles
lists open files, filtered by
.B \-p
(process),
.B \-u
(owner),
.B \-c
(command prefix),
.B \-i
(sockets), and
.B \-t
(terse pids), with
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wide
for unshortened columns.
.B evilfs
prints mounted file systems.
.B evillogs
prints core dump settings, dump reports, and log directories, or only one with
.B \-\-cores
or
.BR \-\-logs .
.B eviliso
prints namespaces
.RB ( \-n ),
cgroups
.RB ( \-c ),
login sessions
.RB ( \-s ),
remote sockets
.RB ( \-r ),
container runtime data
.RB ( \-k ),
.BR \-\-container ,
and
.B \-\-kubernetes
sections, all of them without a selector, with
.B \-a
for detailed rows; the Kubernetes section reads KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST, the
service-account namespace file, and kubepods cgroups.
.B evilss
prints TCP, UDP, and Unix sockets with ss-compatible selectors.
.IP
.B evilio
prints memory, swap, and cumulative disk counters;
.B \-a
adds processor, paging, scheduler, pressure, disk activity, and swap rates
from a one-second sample,
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
(the default) uses binary units, and
.BR \-\-ps ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-p ,
and
.BI \- NUMBER
print per-process counters for every process, a limited table, one process, or
NUMBER rows.
.B evilnet
prints IPv4 and IPv6 addresses;
.B \-a
adds traffic, errors, drops, and TCP counters,
.B \-f
TCP failure and loss counters only, and
.BR \-t ", " \-\-traffic
the traffic table only.
.B evilps
prints processes as a tree;
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-U ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-M ,
and
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wide
show pids, sort numerically, show every field, command lines, owners, CPU,
memory, and full-length lines, and
.BI \- NUMBER
limits the rows.
.IP
For evilio, evilnet, evilps, and evilss,
.BR \-l ", " \-\-live [= \fIN\fR ]
(only
.B \-\-live
for evilss) refreshes every N seconds, 0.5 by default, on the alternate screen
until q (status 0) or an interrupt (status 130); a tall frame scrolls with j,
k, the arrow and page keys, g, G, and the mouse wheel. In evilio, evilnet, and
evilps,
.BR \-\-cumulative [= \fIM\fR ]
(\fB\-C\fR in evilps) reports the change over M seconds, 1 by default, or a
rolling M-second window with
.BR \-\-live .
.B \-\-sort
orders rows by a metric or its shortest unambiguous prefix: pid, read, write,
read-ops, and write-ops for processes; also busy, read-latency,
write-latency, average-queue, queue, errors, and retries for disks; name,
bytes, packets, errors, or drops for traffic; and name, pid, cpu, or memory in
evilps, where s cycles the key and / searches in live view.
.TP
.B file
Classify file kinds and contents.
.BR \-h ", " \-\-no\-dereference
classifies a link and
.BR \-L ", " \-\-dereference
its target,
.BR \-i ", " \-\-regular\-only
skips content tests,
.BR \-m ", " \-\-magic\-file
adds a magic database, and
.BR \-M ", " \-\-magic\-only
uses only the given ones.
.TP
.B find
Walk sorted trees with
.BR \-name ,
.BR \-iname ,
.BR \-type ,
.BR \-maxdepth ,
.BR \-mindepth ,
.BR \-print ,
and
.BR \-print0 .
.B \-xdev
and
.B \-mount
stay on one file system.
.B \-exec
.IR command " ... " ;
runs once per path with {} replaced, and
.B \-exec
.IR command " {} " +
runs on batches of paths.
.TP
.B flock
Run a command while holding a lock on a file or directory; the command inherits
the descriptor.
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nonblock
fails with status 1 instead of waiting, and
.B \-\-transaction\-held\-lock
keeps the lock after the launching process exits.
.TP
.B fuser
Print the pids that use each file, with
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.BR \-u .
.TP
.B getconf
Print a system or string variable, or a path variable for a path; an
unsupported one prints undefined.
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
prints every variable, and
.BR \-v ", " \-\-specification
selects a POSIX environment.
.TP
.B goodcore
Capture a running process with
.BR \-p ,
or package an existing core with its executable with
.BR \-b .
The archive holds the core in dump/core, the executable and its libraries under
root/, and debug-gdb.sh and debug-lldb.sh, which open the core in gdb or lldb
with root/ as the system root and pass their arguments to the debugger.
.TP
.B goodfsw
Report path changes by scanning metadata, at most once per latency period. On
Linux a scan follows an inotify event and a quiet tree is rescanned every 30
periods; elsewhere, or when a watch fails, a scan runs every period. Each
record holds the scan time, a numeric event mask, and the path.
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human\-readable
uses timestamps and event labels, and
.BR \-m ", " \-\-machine\-readable
the numeric form;
.B \-\-timezone
and
.B \-\-precision
format human timestamps.
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-\-event\-flags ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.B \-e
select recursion, appended labels, one file system, one batch, the latency,
and an excluded substring.
.TP
.B goodnode
Print inode identity, allocation, file system capabilities, verification state,
and a CRC32C checksum for each path.
.B \-i
locates an inode beneath the
.B \-r
path, and
.B \-\-verify
runs the native file system verification where supported.
.TP
.B goodstat
Print file metadata as a labeled report.
.B \-L
follows links,
.B \-c
adds a CRC32C checksum, and
.B \-f
a file system capacity report.
.TP
.BR grep ", " egrep ", " fgrep
Print lines matching basic regular expressions, or extended ones with
.B \-E
(egrep), or fixed strings with
.B \-F
(fgrep); the two conflict with status 2.
.B \-e
adds a pattern and
.B \-f
the lines of a file, and with either every operand is a file; a newline
separates patterns.
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-h ,
and
.B \-r
ignore case, invert, match whole lines, number lines, omit file prefixes, and
recurse, and
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
keeps
.B \-r
on one file system.
.B \-c
counts,
.B \-l
lists matching files and overrides
.BR \-c ,
.B \-q
exits 0 at the first match, and
.B \-s
hides unreadable-file messages. In a file with a NUL byte, matches before the
line of the first NUL print, the next one shows a single warning, and later ones
print nothing, unless
.BR \-a ", " \-\-text
treats it as text. In a UTF-8 locale a dot and a bracket expression match one
character, and one byte otherwise. The status is 0 when a line is selected, 1
when none is, and 2 on error.
.IP
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
takes auto (the default), always, or never. GREP_COLORS holds colon-separated
GNU capabilities such as ms=01;31: ms, mt, sl, cx, fn, ln, se, and rv apply,
unknown ones are ignored, and an empty value turns that color off. GREP_COLOR
sets the match color below GREP_COLORS.
.TP
.BR head ", " tail
Print the first or last ten lines.
.B \-n
sets a line count and
.B \-c
a byte count, with an optional suffix: b for 512, k, K, m, M, G, T, P, E, Z, Y,
R, or Q for powers of 1024, and those letters followed by B for powers of 1000
or by iB, as in 1kB and 1KiB. A first argument such as \-5 means
.BR "\-n 5" .
For head, a leading dash prints all but the last N; for tail, a leading plus
starts at line or byte N. Headers appear for several files;
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-\-quiet ,
and
.B \-\-silent
suppress them and
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
forces them, the last winning.
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero\-terminated
uses NUL line ends.
.IP
.B "tail \-f"
keeps printing what is appended to each regular file, and
.B \-F
follows by name, reopening a replaced, removed, or new file, and implies
.BR \-\-retry .
.BI \-\-follow= descriptor|name
selects the mode,
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sleep\-interval
the longest wait between checks (1 second), and
.BI \-\-pid= pid
ends following after that process exits. An interrupted follow exits with
status 130.
.TP
.BR killall ", " pkill
.B killall
signals processes by exact name, and
.B pkill
those whose short command contains the pattern. The signal comes from
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signal
in killall, and from
.B \-\-signal
or
.BI \- SIGNAL
in pkill;
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
lists signals. pkill
.BR \-s ", " \-\-session
takes comma-separated session ids, 0 for its own, and then the pattern is
optional; Windows has no sessions, so 0 is rejected and nothing matches another
id.
.B \-\-inverse
signals what the pattern and sessions do not select. Process 0, the shell, and
the utility itself are never signaled by killall.
.TP
.B less
Show files or standard input full screen on the alternate screen, with keys
from the terminal: space, f, and Page Down forward a page, b and Page Up back,
d and u half a page, j, Down, and Enter one row down, k and Up one row up, g
and Home the top, G and End the bottom,
.BI / pattern
and
.BI ? pattern
extended regular expression searches with n and N, and q to quit or an
interrupt for status 130. The status row shows the file, the visible lines,
and the position. Input loads as needed, up to 128 MiB.
.BR \-N ", " \-\-LINE\-NUMBERS
numbers the lines,
.BR \-S ", " \-\-chop\-long\-lines
cuts long lines instead of wrapping them, and
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
ignores case in searches. Several files follow headers of colons. When output
is not a terminal, the input is copied unchanged.
.TP
.B ln
Create hard links, or symbolic links with
.BR \-s .
.B \-f
removes an existing destination,
.B \-L
follows a link source, and
.B \-P
links the link itself.
.TP
.B logname
Print the login user, which can differ from the effective user that
.B whoami
prints.
.TP
.B ls
List sorted entries with
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-l ,
and
.BR \-h .
.B \-F
appends type indicators,
.B \-p
a slash to directories,
.B \-d
lists directories themselves,
.B \-n
shows numeric ids, and
.B \-g
and
.B \-o
omit the owner or group.
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-S ,
and
.B \-r
sort by time, by size, and in reverse.
