.TP
.B yes
Repeat the joined operands, or y, until output closes.
.SH LINE EDITING
The interactive editor starts in emacs mode;
.B set \-o vi
selects vi mode and
.B set \-o emacs
returns. Input taller than the terminal shows the rows around the caret and
leaves two rows for hints, without the ghost suggestion and right prompt. A
control character is drawn in caret notation, such as ^[ for ESC, and a
filename holding one completes in the $'...' form. Bracketed paste inserts text
without running it.
.PP
Kill commands save the erased text in a 16-entry kill ring for the session,
and consecutive kills join into one entry. Ctrl\-K on an empty line joins the
next line, and on an empty last line removes it. Undo reverts a yank or a
last-word insertion with all its replacements in one step. Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E, or
.B :e
in vi mode, opens the line in VISUAL, EDITOR, or vi; when the editor exits with
status 0 the saved text replaces the line without running it, and otherwise
the line is unchanged and a message appears.
.PP
Ctrl\-Z undoes while the editor collects a line, and stops a foreground program
otherwise. Ctrl\-Shift\-Z redoes when the terminal reports it apart from
Ctrl\-Z; the editor requests such reports through the kitty keyboard protocol
and xterm modifyOtherKeys, which tmux passes on with its
.B extended\-keys
option on.
.B koshconf set editor.request_extended_key_reports off
stops the requests. On the Windows console both keys undo and Ctrl\-^ redoes.
.PP
On a non-empty line, Up recalls only history entries that begin with the text
typed before the first Up, and Down moves back; an empty line steps through all
history. editor.history.arrow_keys_search_by_typed_prefix, on by default,
controls this.
.B koshconf set editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes on
makes a typed (, [, {, or quote insert its closer, step over a typed closer,
and delete an empty pair on Backspace.
.PP
When the caret is on or just after a bracket, the bracket and its partner are
highlighted; a bracket inside quotes, a comment, a here-document, or escaped
never pairs.
.SS Hints
The rows below the input explain the command under the caret under a
.B synopsis
header once the caret is past the command word: up to 16 rows pair the
command, a subcommand such as
.BR "git commit" ,
and each flag with its meaning, the current word marked with
.BR > .
The text comes from the manual page, builtin or koshkit help, alias
definition, or function location. A key waiting for another key, such as
Ctrl\-X or a vi operator, lists the keys that complete it there. The rows are
hidden while a menu or search is open.
.PP
After a short pause, the hint of an external program loads its section 1
manual page, a subcommand page such as
.BR git\-commit (1),
or, for an allowlisted command, its help output.
.BR man ,
.BR manpath ,
and the program run only from an absolute directory owned by the user or root
that no group or other user can write, and never through an empty or relative
PATH element. A keystroke never runs the command itself. A Tab that needs a
load shows
.B loading...
while it waits.
.PP
In the same pause, a line that parses is analyzed as Enter would analyze it,
without following sources, resolving commands, or opening files, and the rows
show the first finding as
.BR error ,
.BR warning ,
or
.BR note .
A line that would not parse shows its first error under
.BR "syntax error" .
The editor submits the whole buffer on Enter with no continuation prompt, so an
open construct is an error even if a later line would close it.
.PP
editor.hints.show_command_synopsis turns off the explanations and their loads,
and editor.hints.show_live_diagnostics the findings and syntax errors; both are
on by default, and turning off both also hides the pending-key rows.
.SS Prompts and title
The terminal title shows the user and directory, or the running foreground
command; output of PROMPT_COMMAND or PS1 can replace the idle title. RPS1, or
RPROMPT when RPS1 is unset or empty, is a right prompt drawn on the first
input row, hidden when there is no room or when it holds a control character
other than a color sequence. A nonempty KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1 or
KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 replaces the prompt or right prompt on a submitted or
interrupted line. See
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.SS Completion
Ghost text offers prefix matches from completion and history; the right arrow
or End at the end of the line accepts it, and Ctrl\-Right or Alt\-F its next
word. Tab also offers smart-case and subsequence matches. An empty command
position offers keywords, builtins, functions, aliases, bundled utilities, and
PATH programs. A directory candidate ends in a slash. The operand of
.B cd
or
.B pushd
completes directories, also under each CDPATH entry unless it is absolute or
starts with a dot or tilde. Targets of
.B make
come from the Makefile, and those after
.B cmake \-\-build
.I dir
.B \-\-target
from
.IR dir /CMakeFiles/TargetDirectories.txt,
without running either program or any $(shell ...) function.
.PP
An explicit Tab first inserts the longest common prefix. A sole candidate
replaces the token, followed by a space as
completion.add_space_after_completed_word selects:
.B off
(the default),
.BR on ,
or
.BR on\-excluding\-trailing\-slash .
Several candidates go to the selector of
.B \-\-tab\-selector
or completion.menu_style.
.PP
The interactive selector draws a menu below the line, headed
.B selecting completions
or, for Ctrl\-R,
.BR "incremental history search" .
Typing narrows the menu: command, path, and koshconf option lists keep the
best of the exact-prefix, smart-case-prefix, and subsequence tiers, and other
lists keep every case-insensitive prefix match. A history entry matches when
the query starts it, appears in it, or appears in it as bytes in order.
Backspace widens the list, and a blank, slash, or erase below the token asks
the completion again after a pause. Accepting a directory opens a menu of its
entries.
.PP
The external selector passes the candidates to
.B KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND
and replaces the token with its output. A dismissed selector leaves the line
alone. A single candidate, or a session without a controlling terminal, uses
the plain listing, and so does an empty command, a command that is not found,
or an exit status other than 0, 1, or 130, after a message.
.PP
The plain selector prints the candidates as a column list, which a terminal
without decoration support always receives; for Ctrl\-R it keeps the
incremental search inside the editor.
.PP
Ctrl\-R searches the history through the selected selector, newest first, with
the line as the query; an accepted entry replaces the line. The search uses a
snapshot of the shared history file and does not change the private history.
.SS Terminal integration
An interactive shell with terminal output writes OSC 133 A, B, C, and D marks
and OSC 7 working directory marks, plus OSC 633 marks when TERM_PROGRAM is
vscode, so terminals can find prompts, commands, statuses, and the directory.
No mark is written when TERM is dumb or
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
is 0.
.SS Emacs mode keys
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-A ", " Home
Move to the start of the line.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-E ", " End
