.TP
.BR gq ", " gqq
Wrap the lines of a motion, or the current line, at editor.wrap_width (80),
keeping the indentation of each paragraph's first line.
.TP
.BR x ", " X ", " D ", " C ", " s ", " S
Delete under or before the cursor, delete or change to the line end, change one
character, or change the line.
.TP
.BI r x ", " ~
Replace the character, or toggle its case and move right.
.TP
.BR p ", " P
Paste after or before the cursor.
.TP
.BR o ", " O ", " J
Open a line below or above, or join the next line with one space.
.TP
.BR { ", " }
Move to the previous or next empty line.
.TP
.BR i ", " a ", " I ", " A ", " R
Insert before or after the cursor, at the first non-blank, or at the line end;
R inserts without overwriting.
.TP
.B .
Repeat the last change other than a yank.
.TP
.BR u ", " Ctrl\-_ ", " Ctrl\-Z
Undo.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-R ", " Ctrl\-^ ", " Ctrl\-Shift\-Z
Redo.
.TP
.BR / ", " ? ", " n ", " N
Search the history, or in
.B slurp
find text forward or backward and repeat the find in either direction.
.TP
.B :
Open the colon command line.
.TP
.BR v ", " V ", " Ctrl\-V
Select characters, lines, or a rectangle.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Ctrl\-D ", " Ctrl\-C ", " Ctrl\-L
Act as in emacs mode.
.SS Vi visual and rectangle keys
Motions move the end of the selection, or the rectangle corner, without a
count. Visual mode accepts
.BR d ,
.B x
(delete),
.B c
(change),
.B y
(yank),
.B gq
(wrap, repeatable with .), and
.B :
(colon commands on the selected lines, shown as :'<,'>). Rectangle mode
accepts
.BR d ,
.B x
(delete), and
.BR I ,
.BR c ,
.B C
(insert at the left column of every line when insert mode ends). Escape,
.B v
or Ctrl\-V, and any other unrecognized key leave the selection, and Enter,
Ctrl\-D, and Ctrl\-C leave it and act as in emacs mode.
.SS Vi colon commands
Enter runs the command, and Escape, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, or Backspace on an empty
line closes it. Unknown commands do nothing.
.TP
.BR :q ", " :q! ", " :quit ", " :wq ", " :wq! ", " :wqa ", " :wqa! ", " :x
Exit with the status of the last command; in
.BR slurp ,
the w forms save first.
.TP
.BR :w ", " :write
Save the file in
.BR slurp .
.TP
.BI :s/ old / new / \fR[\fBg\fR]
Replace the first match of the basic regular expression
.I old
on the caret row, or every match with
.BR g ;
.B :%s
acts on every row. Any punctuation can delimit, and in
.I new
.B &
is the match and
.BR \e1 \- \e9
its groups.
.BR :& ,
.BR :&& ,
and
.B :s
repeat the last substitution,
.B :&&
with its
.B g
flag, and
.B :%&
and
.B :%&&
on every row.
.TP
.BI :set " option" \fR[\fB=\fIvalue\fR|\fB?\fR], " " :set " no" option
Set, show, or turn off a
.B koshconf
option; Tab completes it.
.TP
.BR :save " [\fIoption\fR]"
Write the option, or every option, to
.IR kosh.conf .
.TP
.BR :format ", " :%format
Format the caret row or the buffer as
.B \-\-format
does; a source that does not parse is kept and its error shown.
.TP
.BR :e ", " :edit ", " :u ", " :undo ", " :red ", " :redo
Edit in an external editor, undo, or redo.
.TP
.BR :h ", " :help
List the colon commands until the next key.
.SS Emacs M\-x commands
Tab completes a command, Enter runs it, and Escape, Ctrl\-C, Ctrl\-D, or
Backspace on an empty minibuffer closes it. The hint rows list the matching
commands, each with what it does when six or fewer match. The commands are
.B help
(list the commands),
.B replace\-string
and
.B replace\-regexp
(replace every match in the buffer, with \e& and \e1\-\e9 in a regexp
replacement),
.B repeat
(as Ctrl\-X z),
.BR set\-option ,
.BR get\-option ,
and
.B save\-option
(as :set and :save),
.B format
and
.B format\-buffer
(as :format and :%format),
.B fill\-paragraph
(as gq on the paragraph),
.B isearch\-forward
and
.BR isearch\-backward ,
.B kill\-kosh
(as Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-C),
.B undo
and
.BR undo\-redo ,
and the commands of the matching keys:
.BR beginning\-of\-line ,
.BR end\-of\-line ,
.BR forward\-word ,
.BR backward\-word ,
.BR scroll\-down\-command ,
.BR scroll\-up\-command ,
.BR beginning\-of\-history ,
.BR end\-of\-history ,
.BR delete\-char ,
.BR backward\-delete\-char ,
.BR kill\-word ,
.BR backward\-kill\-word ,
.BR kill\-line ,
.BR unix\-line\-discard ,
.BR yank ,
.BR yank\-last\-arg ,
.BR transpose\-chars ,
.BR transpose\-words ,
.BR upcase\-word ,
.BR downcase\-word ,
.BR capitalize\-word ,
.BR delete\-horizontal\-space ,
and
.BR clear\-screen .
.SS Multi-cursor keys
Up, Down, Ctrl\-P, and Ctrl\-N add a cursor on the row above or below.
Left, Right, Home, End, Ctrl\-B, Ctrl\-F, Ctrl\-A, and Ctrl\-E move every
cursor, and typed characters and Backspace act at every cursor. Ctrl\-G,
Escape, and any other unrecognized key leave the mode, and Enter, Ctrl\-D, and
Ctrl\-C leave it and act as in emacs mode.
.SS Completion menu keys
.TP
.BR Down ", " Ctrl\-N ", " Up ", " Ctrl\-P ", " Shift\-Tab
Highlight the next or previous row, wrapping around.
.TP
.B Tab
Accept the row, opening a directory.
.TP
.B Right
Accept the row and offer the completions that follow it.
.TP
.B Enter
Close the menu and run the line as it stands.
.TP
.BR \fIcharacter\fR ", " Backspace
Narrow or widen the rows.
.TP
.B Escape
Close the menu and keep the edited line.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-G
Close the menu and restore the original line, as does any other unrecognized
key.
.TP
.I other
Close the menu, then act on the line.
.SS History search keys
In the interactive selector, Enter and Tab accept the entry without running
it, Escape, Ctrl\-G, and other unrecognized keys restore the line, and the
other keys act as in the completion menu. The plain selector accepts:
.TP
.BR Down ", " Ctrl\-N ", " Ctrl\-R
Step to an older match.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Ctrl\-P ", " Ctrl\-F ", " Shift\-Tab
Step to a newer match.
.TP
.BR \fIcharacter\fR ", " Backspace
Extend or shorten the query.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " Tab
Accept the match without running it.
.TP
.BR Escape ", " Ctrl\-G
Cancel and restore the line, as does any other unrecognized key.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-C ", " Ctrl\-D
Cancel, restore the line, and act on it.
.TP
.I other
Accept the match, then act on the line.
.SH JOB CONTROL
Every stage of an asynchronous pipeline belongs to one job, and POSIX stages
share a process group. The final stage determines $!, the status, and the job
identity, except that under pipefail the rightmost failing stage may determine
the status. A stopped stage is recorded even when it is not the last, and
.B fg
and
.B bg
resume every stage.
.PP
An ampersand ends a whole and-or list, so
.B a && b || c &
runs as one background job whose variable and directory changes stay in the
job. In the Bash and
.B sh
moods an asynchronous simple command forks before expanding its words,
assignments, and redirections, as Bash and dash do, so an expansion error fails
the job with status 1, or 2 in the
.B sh
mood. The
.B kosh
mood expands the words before starting the job.
.PP
.B jobs
and job notifications print one line per job in the Bash columns: the number
and current or previous mark, the process with
.BR \-l ,
the state, and the command. A job ended by a signal shows its description,
such as Terminated, and
.B wait
reports 128 plus the signal number; an exited job shows Done, or Exit and its
status.
.PP
A job reported by
.BR wait ,
.BR jobs ,
or a notification leaves the job table, and a later
.B wait
on its process returns the remembered status; the shell remembers the 1024
most recent statuses.
.B wait
reports 127 and a diagnostic for a process that is not its child or a job that
does not exist, and with several operands the last decides the status. A
subshell, a pipeline stage, an asynchronous command, and a coprocess cannot
wait for a job of their parent and report 127. In the
.B sh
mood a job number matching no job reports 2 from
.B wait
and
.BR jobs .
.PP
.B wait \-n
returns the status of the next job to finish among the operands, or among all
jobs, and 127 when none is left.
.BI "wait \-p " var
stores the process whose status is returned in var, or unsets var. A stopped
job returns 128 plus the stop signal, and
.B wait \-n
skips it.
.B wait \-f
waits until each job terminates.
.PP
A coprocess is one asynchronous job and sets $!. Its descriptors are above 9,
close across an exec, and are not passed to subshells. When it finishes, the
shell closes them and unsets NAME and NAME_PID, as Bash does.
.SH SIGNALS
The main shell ignores SIGPIPE, and a forked child restores the default, so a
producer killed by SIGPIPE returns status 141. On Windows a background process
runs in its own console process group.
.PP
The CHLD trap runs once for every child the shell reaps, at the next command
boundary; under
.B set \-m
a job that stops counts too. When a trapped signal arrives while
.B wait
blocks,
.B wait
returns 128 plus the signal number and the job keeps running.
.PP
As process 1, the shell reaps orphaned children it did not start, before each
command and while it waits. On SIGTERM it sends SIGTERM and then SIGCONT to
every other process, interrupts the running command, waits for every remaining
child, and exits with status 143.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A diagnostic gives the file, line, and column of the failing command, quotes
the source line, and marks the column with a caret. Text built at run time is
its own source: a location in an eval string appears as
.BR eval:2:7: ,
in a trap action as
.BR trap:1:5: ,
and in a
.B \-c
string as
.BR \-c:1:5: ;
standard input and interactive lines have no label. Each function call,
sourced file, eval, trap action, subshell, and command, function, or process
substitution between the top level and the failing command adds a backtrace
frame beginning with
.BR trace: ,
and a repeated frame is printed once with a repeat count. With several
.B \-c
strings or files, the frame pointing into the command line is printed once
after the diagnostics of that string or file. Diagnostics read the same in
every mood.
.PP
After a parse error the parser skips only the failed statement, so lint, the
language server, and a script report every syntax error in one run. A construct
that the mood lacks, such as
.B [[
in the
.B sh
mood, is reported and parsed as usual.
.PP
Analysis follows static source operands into readable files. In the
.B kosh
mood strict and lenient findings reject the script, including the expansion of
a variable that is never assigned, a call without arguments to a function that
uses them, and a call to a function before its definition. An assignment in a
function to a variable not declared
.B local
is reported; in the
.B sh
mood, which has no
.BR local ,
only a variable first set in a function and also used outside it is reported.
.PP
A comment such as
.B # shellcheck disable=SC2086,unquoted-command-substitution
suppresses analysis checks: a numeric code suppresses every variant under it,
a slug only its own variant, and the value may be
.B all
or a range such as
.BR SC2000\-SC2100 .
Before the first command it covers the file, and later it covers the next
and-or command. It does not suppress parser or runtime diagnostics.
.PP
An unavailable command or path is reported through its first unavailable
component when that can be identified. An internal error stops the shell with
an
.B error:
line and
.B note:
lines identifying the process and build, and the shell aborts.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
On Windows a variable holds at most 32,766 UTF-16 units, and a child receives
a longer value truncated.
.TP
.B KOSH_FLAGS
Default command-line options. See
.BR INVOCATION .
.TP
.B KOSH_ANALYSIS
The analysis settings changed with
.BR set ,
passed to launched shells as words:
.B mimicry
for
.BR \-I ,
.BI warnings= N
for the
.B \-W
level,
.B no\-annoying
for optimizer.show_annoying_tier off, and
.B no\-diagnostics
for optimizer.analyze_before_running off. Each
.B set
rewrites it, and it is removed when empty. An explicit
.B \-W
replaces the inherited level.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_FILE
The history file, ~/.kosh_history by default. A record over 65535 bytes is
skipped on reading. HISTFILE has no effect.
.TP
.B KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE
The number of history events kept and stored, 4096 by default; 0 disables
history.
.IP
A KOSH_HISTORY_FILE or KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE inherited from the environment
overrides editor.history.file_path or editor.history.max_entries, and either
is exported only when inherited or exported explicitly.
.TP
.B KOSH_DIRECTORY_HISTORY
The file of visited directories that z ranks, ~/.kosh_directory_history by
default.
.TP
.B KOSH_CALC_HISTORY
The calc history file, ~/.kosh_calc_history by default.
.TP
.B KOSH_SHELL_INTEGRATION
A value of 0 turns off the terminal integration marks.
.TP
.BR KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_COMMAND ", " KOSH_FZF_COMPLETION_OPTS
The external selector program, fzf by default, and its options, by default
\-\-multi \-\-layout=reverse \-\-height=~40% \-\-min\-height=3, split on blanks
without expansion.
.TP
.BR FCEDIT ", " EDITOR ", " VISUAL
FCEDIT, then EDITOR, selects the editor of
.BR fc ;
VISUAL, then EDITOR, that of Ctrl\-X Ctrl\-E.
.TP
.BR ENV ", " BASH_ENV
Startup files, as described under
.BR "STARTUP FILES" .
.TP
.B PATH
The colon-separated command search path. An empty element, an empty value, and
an unset PATH search the current directory. On Windows the separator is a
semicolon and an empty element is skipped.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " PWD ", " OLDPWD ", " CDPATH
HOME supplies home paths, PWD and OLDPWD hold the logical current and previous
directories, and CDPATH the search path for a relative
.B cd
operand. A relative directory resolves against the logical PWD, and
.B cd ..
removes the last logical component unless
.B cd \-P
or the physical option resolves links first. A logical PWD is accepted only
when it is absolute and identifies the current directory.
.TP
.BR SHELL ", " SHLVL
SHELL is the executable, or the invocation path when unset. A child shell
increments SHLVL.
.TP
.B IFS
The field separators and the joiner of "$*", by default space, tab, and
newline.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
The initial terminal size of an interactive shell.
.TP
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
A command run before each prompt, or, as an indexed array, each nonempty
element in order. Each sees the status and $_ of the previous command, which
the hook does not change. The prompt escapes \eg, \eL, and \eM cover its usual
uses without running a command.
.TP
.BR PS0 ", " PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS3 ", " PS4
The pre-execution, primary, continuation, select, and xtrace prompts. PS0 is
empty, PS2 "> ", PS3 "#? ", and PS4 "+ " by default, and the first byte of PS4
repeats once per enclosing substitution, eval, source, and trap action. The
default PS1 shows the git branch with ahead and behind counts, the user, host,
and directory, and a bullet.
.IP
The escapes are \eu, \eh, \eH, \ek, \eK, \ew, \eW, \eP, \eg, \e$, \e?, \ej,
\eL, \eM, \e., \et, \eT, \e@, \eA, \ed, \eD{format}, \es, \ev, \eV, \e!, \e#,
\el, \en, \er, \ee, \ea, \e[, \e], \e\e, and \ennn (a byte in one to three
octal digits). \ek and \eK are \eh and \eH, colored on a color terminal. \es is
the basename of the invocation path without a login dash or .exe. \ev is the
major and minor version and \eV adds the patch. \e! is the history number of
the next command, and \e# the command number, counting from 1 for each line
read from a terminal, script, or standard input. \el is the last component of
the terminal of standard input, or tty. \eD{format} uses strftime. \eL is the
duration of the last command, empty under 5 milliseconds. \eM is the resident
memory the last command used: the highest peak of its processes and their
descendants, or the growth of the shell during the command if larger, and
empty when the shell only reused memory. \e. is a bullet colored by the last
status: white after 0, bold red after 1, and on a truecolor terminal shifting
toward magenta in four steps for higher statuses.
.IP
Text inserted by an escape is never expanded, and the user, host, directory,
branch, shell, and version draw a control byte in caret notation and a byte
that is not UTF-8 as \exHH. A raw 001 or 002 byte in PS0, PS1, RPS1,
KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1, or KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1 delimits an invisible region, as
\e[ and \e] do.
.TP
.BR RPS1 ", " RPROMPT
The right prompt, expanded like PS1; RPROMPT applies when RPS1 is unset or
empty. Blanks at either end are dropped. An interactive shell started with
neither set uses a dim "\eL \eM".
.TP
.B KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1
When nonempty, the prompt that replaces PS1 on a line that Enter submits or
Ctrl\-C interrupts; KOSH_TRANSIENT_PS1='\e$ ' leaves "$ " before each command
in the scrollback.
.TP
.B KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1
The same for the right prompt. An interactive shell started without it sets it
to \e[\e], which erases the right prompt; KOSH_TRANSIENT_RPS1='\eL' keeps each
duration.
.TP
.BR KOSH_WELCOME ", " KOSH_FAREWELL
Messages printed with the shell prefix at interactive startup and exit. An
interactive shell sets greetings unless the environment holds them, so an
exported empty value, or one of zero display width, disables them.
.TP
.B BASH_XTRACEFD
The xtrace descriptor; standard error when unset or invalid.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " COLORTERM ", " NO_COLOR
TERM selects terminal capabilities on POSIX. COLORTERM containing truecolor or
24bit selects 24-bit colors, and TERM containing 256color or direct selects
256 colors; other terminals draw basic colors. A nonempty NO_COLOR disables
prompt, diagnostic, koshkit cat, and editor color.
.TP
.B MANPATH
The manual paths that completion and hints search.
.TP
.B OPTERR
A value of 0, or a leading colon in the option string, silences
.B getopts
diagnostics.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The directory for temporary files, /tmp by default.
.TP
.BR PIPESTATUS ", " REPLY ", " OPTIND ", " OPTARG ", " TIMEFORMAT
Pipeline statuses, read and select input, getopts state, and time format.
.TP
.BR KOSH ", " KOSH_VERSION ", " KOSH_COMMIT ", " KOSH_BUILD_MODE ", " KOSH_OS
The invocation path, version, build commit, build mode, and build host. They
are never exported, and an inherited value is replaced.
.TP
.B KOSH_IDENTITY
The read-only, exported CRC-32 of the running executable, in lowercase hex. An
inherited value is discarded.
.TP
.B KOSH_GIT_BRANCH
The branch of the repository holding the working directory, a short hash for a
detached HEAD, or empty. An assignment overrides it. The search stops at
GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES.
.TP
.BR KOSH_GIT_AHEAD ", " KOSH_GIT_BEHIND
The unpushed and unpulled commit counts against the upstream, empty when zero
or unavailable.
.TP
.BR KOSH_PS1_ *
The current text of each prompt escape; assignments are ignored. They are
USER (\eu), HOST (\eh), HOST_FULL (\eH), CWD (\ew), CWD_BASE (\eW), CWD_SHORT
(\eP), GIT_BRANCH (\eg), PROMPT_SIGN (\e$), STATUS (\e?), JOBS (\ej), DURATION
(\eL), MEMORY (\eM), DOT (\e.), TIME (\et), TIME_12 (\eT), TIME_AMPM (\e@),
TIME_SHORT (\eA), DATE (\ed), SHELL (\es), VERSION (\ev), VERSION_FULL (\eV),
TTY (\el), HISTORY_NUMBER (\e!), and COMMAND_NUMBER (\e#).
.TP
.B KOSHCONF
The current mood and interactive options in the encoding of
.BR kosh (5).
Assignments are discarded, and the shell never exports it by itself. A value in
the environment at startup is applied after the settings files and removed, so
.B ssh host KOSHCONF=\(dq$KOSHCONF\(dq kosh
carries the settings to a remote shell.
.TP
.B KOSH_ANSI_*
Terminal escapes in BLACK, RED, GREEN, YELLOW, BLUE, MAGENTA, CYAN, WHITE, their
BRIGHT_ forms, BOLD, DIM, and RESET, empty when color is disabled. An explicit
assignment wins.
.TP
.B LINENO
The line of the running command; inside a substitution, the file line of the
command that expands it.
.TP
.BR RANDOM ", " SRANDOM ", " SECONDS ", " EPOCHSECONDS ", " EPOCHREALTIME
Random numbers, elapsed time, and wall time. Assigning SECONDS moves its base,
assigning RANDOM seeds it reproducibly, and unsetting either makes it ordinary.
.TP
.BR BASHPID ", " PPID ", " UID ", " EUID ", " GROUPS ", " HOSTNAME ", " HOSTTYPE ", " MACHTYPE ", " OSTYPE
The current process, parent, ids, groups, host, and platform. $$ stays the
original shell process.
.TP
.B HISTCMD
The history number of the current command, 0 in a non-interactive shell.
Assignments are discarded.
.TP
.BR SHELLOPTS ", " BASHOPTS ", " BASH_SUBSHELL ", " BASH_ARGV0 ", " BASH_EXECUTION_STRING ", " BASH_MONOSECONDS
The read-only set and shopt option lists, the subshell depth, argument zero,
the command string, and a monotonic millisecond count.
.TP
.BR COPROC ", " COPROC_PID
The descriptors and process of a coprocess without NAME.
.TP
.B BASH_ALIASES
The aliases. Assigning an element defines an alias; unsetting an element does
nothing, and unsetting the array disconnects it.
.TP
.BR BASH_ARGC ", " BASH_ARGV
The argument count of each recorded frame, and the arguments in reverse order,
innermost first. Under
.B extdebug
function and source calls are recorded, and otherwise function calls are not.
Assignments do nothing, and unset and local fail.
.TP
.B DIRSTACK
The current directory followed by the saved stack. Assigning an existing index
other than 0 changes that entry; other assignments and element unsets do
nothing, and unsetting the array disconnects it.
.TP
.BR FUNCNAME ", " BASH_SOURCE ", " BASH_LINENO ", " BASH_COMMAND
The call stack and current command.
.TP
.B BASH_REMATCH
The groups of the last [[ =~ ]] match.
.TP
.BR BASH ", " BASH_VERSION ", " BASH_VERSINFO
A Bash identity, set when the mood or an initialized mood is
.B bash
or
.BR bash-posix .
.TP
.BR COMP_WORDS ", " COMP_CWORD ", " COMP_LINE ", " COMP_POINT ", " COMP_WORDBREAKS ", " COMPREPLY
The words, cursor word index, line, cursor byte offset, and word-break bytes
for a completion function, which writes COMPREPLY. Trailing spaces are removed
from each entry, and the spec's
.BR \-X ,
.BR \-P ,
and
.B \-S
apply. With the filenames option a slash is appended to each directory.
.PP
The variables from RANDOM through FUNCNAME are unavailable in the
.B sh
mood.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell exits with the status of the last command. A command path that is a
directory, or ends in a separator and is not a directory, returns 126, and a
script file that cannot be opened returns 127. A syntax error in a script, a
.B \-c
string, an eval, or a sourced file returns 2, as does a usage error. In the
Bash moods an expansion error that ends the shell, such as an unset parameter
under nounset, ${name:?}, or a bad substitution, returns 127 from a
.B \-c
string, as in Bash, and 1 otherwise. A child killed by SIGPIPE returns 141.
.PP
.BR \-\-lint ,
.BR \-\-format ,
and
.B \-\-apply
return 0 when every input succeeds, 1 when an input cannot be read, parsed,
formatted, or replaced or analysis reports an error, and 2 for an invalid
option combination. Warning-only findings return 0.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_history
The default history file.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_directory_history
The default directory history of z.
.TP
.I ~/.kosh_calc_history
The default calc history.
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf ", " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf ", " ~/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh
The settings files and the rc script, described in
.BR kosh (5).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (5),
.BR bash (1),
.BR dash (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR fish (1),
.BR busybox (1)
